{"id": "77:2", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "loquar propositiónes ab inítio", "portuguese": "falarei o que foi proposto desde o princípio", "problem": "propositiones is a noun (LXX problemata: riddles, dark sayings, set questions), parallel to parabolis; rendering it as a relative clause 'what was proposed' turns the noun into an event and blurs the parallel.", "fix": "falarei proposições desde o princípio"}]},
{"id": "77:17", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "in iram excitavérunt Excélsum", "portuguese": "despertaram a ira do Excelso", "problem": "Latin object is the Most High, stirred to anger; the Portuguese makes 'ira' the object. Same sense, but it departs from the construction the translation keeps elsewhere (77:40, 77:58 'incitaram-no à ira').", "fix": "incitaram à ira o Excelso no lugar sem água"}]},
{"id": "77:35", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "Deus adjútor est eórum", "portuguese": "Deus é o seu auxílio", "problem": "adjutor is an agent noun (helper), parallel to redemptor; 'auxílio' abstracts the person into the help.", "fix": "Deus é o seu auxiliador"}]},
{"id": "77:38b", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "Et abundávit ut avérteret iram suam", "portuguese": "E muitas vezes desviou a sua ira", "problem": "'muitas vezes' follows the Hebrew/Douay ('many a time') rather than the Latin, which says he abounded (was lavish) so as to turn away his wrath; the image of abundance and the purpose/result clause are lost.", "fix": "E foi abundante em desviar a sua ira"}]},
{"id": "77:48", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "possessiónem eórum igni", "portuguese": "a sua posse ao fogo", "problem": "Portuguese 'posse' normally denotes the act or right of possessing, not the thing possessed (here: livestock/goods, cf. LXX hyparxin).", "fix": "e a sua possessão ao fogo"}]},
{"id": "77:57", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "quemádmodum patres eórum convérsi sunt in arcum pravum", "portuguese": "assim como os pais deles, converteram-se num arco torto", "problem": "In the Latin, 'patres eorum' is the grammatical subject of 'conversi sunt' inside the quemadmodum clause; the comma detaches it and makes the present generation the subject, resolving an ambiguity the Latin keeps (LXX reads the same way as the Latin).", "fix": "assim como os pais deles se converteram num arco torto"}]},
{"id": "77:72", "severity": "minor", "issues": [{"latin": "in intelléctibus mánuum suárum", "portuguese": "com o entendimento das suas mãos", "problem": "Latin plural 'intellectibus' (LXX synesesin) rendered singular.", "fix": "e com os entendimentos das suas mãos os guiou"}]}
],
"overall": "A faithful, close rendering of the whole psalm that keeps the Septuagintal oddities (unicorns, mosca canina, vento sudoeste, arco torto, the Latin tenses in 77:34 and 77:64); no verse says something the Latin does not say. The findings are all minor: one place follows the Hebrew over the Latin (77:38b 'muitas vezes'), a few nouns abstracted or reshaped (77:2, 77:35, 77:48), one punctuation choice that resolves an ambiguity (77:57), and one number shift (77:72). Pointing marks match the Latin in every verse."}
